  [1mOne Codex finding was declined, not fixed.[0m All three round-1 lenses reported the same high-severity claim:
  [3m"greet.test.js has no test for empty-string input... add a test that calls [38;5;153mgreet('')[39m."[0m That test already existed —
  [38;5;153mgreet.test.js:11[39m is literally [38;5;153massert.strictEqual(greet(''), 'Hello, Guest!')[39m. I confirmed it three ways before
  declining: reading the file at head, my own [38;5;153mnode --test[39m run (the passing test is named "greet handles empty input
  gracefully"), and the independent Claude review citing the same lines. Applying the recommendation would have
  duplicated an existing assertion. The final opus reviewer formed the same view independently and endorsed the decline.
  Codex round 2 did not contest it.

  Worth noting: three lenses agreeing was [3mnot[0m three checks — they reproduced one false claim. What caught it was
  re-running the covering command against head.

  [1mTwo Important findings I did not fix — they need your call[0m

  Both conflict with the plan's binding constraints, so they're yours to decide rather than mine to fix:

  [1mIMP-1 — the plan's Goal isn't actually delivered.[0m [38;5;153mplan.md:5[39m says "The app can greet a provided name with custom
  formatting." It can't. [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m still requires [38;5;153m./utils[39m and calls the old fixed-format [38;5;153mgreet('world')[39m. The new
  module's only consumer is its own test file. This isn't an implementation defect — the plan's file list is exhaustive
  and forbids touching [38;5;153msrc/[39m, so this was the only compliant outcome. No gate in the train caught it, because each was
  scoped to the diff rather than the Goal.

  [1mIMP-2 — two divergent [38;5;153mgreet[39m exports now coexist.[0m [38;5;153msrc/utils.js:1[39m still exports an undefended [38;5;153mgreet(name)[39m
  ([38;5;153mutils.greet(undefined)[39m → [38;5;153m'Hello, undefined!'[39m, exactly what the new one prevents). Nothing marks either as canonical.
  The risk is silent mis-selection: a future [38;5;153mrequire('./utils')[39m gets the unsafe one and fails as a cosmetically wrong
  string, not an exception.

